  "documentation": {
    "range": "<em>Serrasalmus rhombeus </em>occurs in the Amazon and Orinoco River basins, northern and eastern Guiana Shield, and  northeastern Brazilian coastal rivers, in Bolivia,  Brazil, Colombia, Ecuador, French Guiana, Guyana, Peru, Suriname and  Venezuela (Froese and Pauly 2019, Fricke <em>et al. </em>2020).",
    "population": "This species is considered a common fish in the Amazonian basin and is generally stable.",
    "habitats": "<span>This species occurs in the rapids but is also captured in deep zones of main  rivers with the use of fish bait. It is essentially a carnivore, feeds on  small fish, crabs, mammals, lizards and coleopteran insects.  Its  opportunistic behaviour allows it to adapt to various biotopes (Planquette <em>et al. </em>1996).   It is timid and not aggressive, but it possesses powerful dentition  that can cause serious bites, and is therefore, potentially dangerous.   Import is prohibited in some areas.",
    "threats": "There are no apparent threats to this species. However, since this is a migrant species, dam construction could affect the species in the near future.",
    "measures": "This species does not have any conservation measures in place. There is a lack of basic information for this species, regarding its habitat and population trends; further research is therefore required.",
    "use_trade": "This species is probably caught for local consumption. In Brazil, the  species is allowed in ornamental fish trade (IBAMA 2012).",
    "rationale": "The species is widely distributed in the Amazon and Orinoco basins with many occurrences, especially in Brazil. It is therefore assessed as Least Concern.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
